{
  "id": "tpl-6d-ab-split-db",
  "number": 0,
  "name": "A/B Split · Dumbbells & Bodyweight · 6 Days",
  "method": "flat-pyramid",
  "start_date": "2026-01-05",
  "weeks": 6,
  "origin": "pt",
  "summary": "Six weeks, six sessions needing only dumbbells, an adjustable bench and your bodyweight: chest & back, shoulders & arms, and legs, each twice a week. Same weight on every set. A days heavier, B days lighter. Week 6 is lighter.",
  "insights": [
    {
      "title": "Goal",
      "body": "Build muscle with dumbbells and bodyweight for people who like to train almost every day. Each muscle is trained twice a week, and most get at least one exercise that loads it in a deep stretch, like flies, pullovers and incline curls."
    },
    {
      "title": "How it works",
      "body": "Each day has one job: chest and back (plus abs), then shoulders and arms, then legs, twice a week. Every set of an exercise uses the same weight and reps. Paired exercises (same letter) are supersets: first exercise, about 10 seconds, second exercise, then the full rest."
    },
    {
      "title": "A days vs B days",
      "body": "A days are heavier: 8 reps on the first lifts, 10 on other compounds, 12 on isolation and abs, and 15 on lateral raises, rear delts and calves. B days repeat the same exercises with lighter weights for 10, 12, 15 and 20 reps. Track A and B days separately."
    },
    {
      "title": "How to progress",
      "body": "When every set reaches the target reps, add the smallest dumbbell step. If your heaviest dumbbells get too easy, add 2-5 reps per set or slow the lowering to 3 seconds. Week 1 stop about 3 reps before failure, week 2 about 2, weeks 3-4 about 1-2, week 5 about 1."
    },
    {
      "title": "What to expect",
      "body": "Week 1 should feel easy while you find your weights. Weeks 2-5 should bring more reps or weight. Shoulder days come right after chest and back, so pressing may feel slightly weaker; that is normal. Week 6 is lighter: same exercises and weights, half the sets (round up), about 3 reps before failure."
    },
    {
      "title": "Key tips",
      "body": "Six days is demanding: sleep well and take a full rest day each week. If you feel run down for more than a few days, use the week-6 approach early. Set the bench angle before each superset. If a movement hurts, stop it and see a qualified professional."
    }
  ],
